---
/**
 * ACADEMIC STRUCTURE · 02 — THE FIVE STAGES
 *
 * Five rows down a spine, each a node and a panel. Fourteen years have an ORDER,
 * and this is the shape that says so without asking the copy to fit in a column.
 *
 * ⚠ THIS REPLACED A FIVE-ACROSS GRID, and the reason was measured rather than
 * felt. In that layout, at a 1280 container:
 *
 *   · THE BODY COPY SET AT 17–21 CHARACTERS PER LINE. Comfortable reading is
 *     45–75. Each card was ~208px wide and ~165px of that was text — narrower
 *     than a newspaper classified. Five columns of it is genuinely hard work,
 *     and it was the root cause of everything else below.
 *   · PILLS WRAPPED. "KIDS entrepreneurship, Class VII" and "Full laboratory
 *     programme" broke to two lines INSIDE a rounded pill, which reads as
 *     broken rather than as designed.
 *   · 28–96px OF DEAD SPACE floated mid-card, different on every one: the cards
 *     stretched to the tallest and `margin-top: auto` dumped all the slack in
 *     one place.
 *   · THE CONNECTOR WAS 40×2px. At that size it did not read as a journey; it
 *     read as dust in the gaps.
 *
 * None of that is fixable by adjusting spacing. Five columns was the decision
 * that had to change.
 *
 * ═══ HOW THE ROW IS BUILT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * node · panel, and the panel splits again into copy and a side column carrying
 * the focus tags and the milestone. That second split is what stops a full-width
 * row from setting one very long line: the copy takes a measure it can be read
 * at, and the width left over does useful work instead of being margin.
 *
 * ═══ THE CARDS STACK ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * Each card pins a little lower than the one before, so scrolling collects the
 * five into a deck instead of carrying them off the top of the screen.
 *
 * IT IS `position: sticky`, WITH NO JAVASCRIPT AT ALL. The obvious build is a
 * GSAP ScrollTrigger per card and it buys nothing here: sticky is the browser's
 * own implementation of exactly this, it runs on the compositor, it cannot
 * desynchronise from the scroll position, and with a blocked script the section
 * is still a perfectly good list.
 *
 * ⚠ AND IT COST THE SPINE. A side rail and a stack cannot both work — sticky
 * pins the whole row, so the five nodes piled at a 23px step showed 23px
 * slivers of one another and read as a rendering fault. The node moved into the
 * card, the rail and its drawn spine went, and the IntersectionObserver that
 * drew that spine went with them rather than being left running with nothing to
 * do. The stack is the sequence device now, and the "01/05" on each card says
 * where in the five you are — which a reader can no longer count once the cards
 * overlap.
 *
 * THE ROW CLASS IS `astg`, NOT `asr`. Streams already owns `.asr` for its
 * photograph cards. Astro's scoping keeps the two stylesheets apart, so nothing
 * was broken — but a page with two different `.asr` blocks is a trap for the
 * next person reading it, and for any script that queries the page rather than
 * the component.
 *
 * ⚠ NO AGES. The school's age criteria live in a Drive-hosted prospectus and are
 * not ours to estimate, so every stage is identified by its class band. Asset
 * request A8, and it is the reason `classes` exists in the data at all.
 */
import AcademicGlyph from '../AcademicGlyph.astro';
import { getAcademicTopic } from '../../../lib/cms';

const ac = await getAcademicTopic('/academics/structure/');
const stages = ac.section('stages').points;
---

<section
  class="asst"
  id="stages"
  aria-labelledby="asst-title"
  style={`--stack-n: ${stages.length - 1};`}
>
  <span class="asst__dots" aria-hidden="true"></span>

  <div class="u-container">
    <div class="asst__head">
      <p class="asst__eyebrow">The five stages</p>
      <h2 class="asst__h" id="asst-title" data-split>A closer look at each stage</h2>
    </div>

    <ol class="asst__list">
      {
        stages.map((s, i) => (
          <li
            class="astg"
            id={s.key}
            style={`--acc: var(--as-${s.accent}); --acc-wash: var(--as-${s.accent}-wash); --i: ${i};`}
          >
            <article class="astg__panel">
              {/* THE STUB. A ticket's counterfoil: the glyph, the class band and
                  the position in the run, torn off down a perforation from the
                  itemised half. It is also what fixes the plain white card the
                  receipt had been — the left third now carries the stage's
                  colour instead of being empty. */}
              <div class="astg__stub">
                <span class="astg__node" aria-hidden="true">
                  <AcademicGlyph name={s.glyph} size={22} />
                </span>
                <span class="astg__classes t-tabular">{s.classes}</span>
                {/* Decorative: the ordered list already carries the sequence,
                    and hearing "01 Pre-Primary" for five rows is it read twice.
                    It earns its place once the cards stack, because a reader
                    looking at the top of the deck can no longer count what is
                    under it. */}
                <span class="astg__n t-tabular" aria-hidden="true">
                  {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}<i>/{String(stages.length).padStart(2, '0')}</i>
                </span>
              </div>

              <div class="astg__main">
                <h3 class="astg__h">{s.stage}</h3>
                <p class="astg__b">{s.body}</p>
              </div>

              {/* The itemised half. Pills became line items with dotted leaders:
                  three chips of unequal width read as three unrelated labels,
                  while three ruled lines read as a list of what this stage
                  covers — which is what they are. */}
              <div class="astg__side">
                <p class="astg__l">Focus</p>
                <ul class="astg__items">
                  {s.focus.map((f) => (
                    <li class="astg__item">
                      <span class="astg__nm">{f}</span>
                      <span class="astg__lead" aria-hidden="true" />
                      <span class="astg__mk" aria-hidden="true" />
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>

                <p class="astg__ms">
                  <span class="astg__ms-l">Milestone</span>
                  <span class="astg__ms-v">{s.milestone}</span>
                </p>
              </div>
            </article>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ⚠ NO `overflow: hidden` ON THIS SECTION, EVER. It was added here for one
     build to clip the dot field and it silently killed the stack: every card
     stopped pinning and scrolled straight past — measured, the five reported
     tops of -915, -710, -351, 8 and 367 where they should have been a tidy pile.
     `overflow` on ANY ancestor makes that ancestor the sticky scroll container,
     and a non-scrolling box is one a sticky child can never travel within.
     It is also not needed: the dot layer is `position: absolute; inset: 0`
     against this relatively-positioned box, so it already cannot paint outside
     it. `isolation` is what the negative z-index needs, and isolation does not
     touch sticky. */
  .asst {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    padding-block: clamp(48px, 7vh, 84px) clamp(56px, 8vh, 100px);
    background: var(--as-ground);
    --node: clamp(48px, 4vw, 60px);

    /* ── THE STACK ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Where the first card comes to rest, and how far each one after it sits
       below the last. The step is what leaves a strip of every card in the pile
       visible instead of burying them. */
    --card-h: 320px;
    --stack-step: clamp(18px, 1.8vw, 27px);

    /* IT RESTS IN THE MIDDLE OF THE SCREEN, NOT UNDER THE MASTHEAD. Pinning the
       first card just below the header put the whole deck in the top third with
       half a screen of empty page beneath it — measured, the pile's centre sat
       142px above the viewport's at 1600×900. The cards were arriving at the
       edge of vision rather than where a reader is looking.

       Centring needs the pile's height, which is the tallest card plus every
       step below it. CSS cannot measure the card, so --card-h approximates it
       per breakpoint: measured 254–291px on desktop, 316 at 768, 363–387 at 390.
       Being a few pixels out shifts the deck by half that, which is invisible.

       --stack-n comes off the section as stages.length - 1, so adding a sixth
       stage re-centres the pile instead of pushing it off the bottom.

       THE max() IS A FLOOR, NOT A PREFERENCE. On a short window the centred
       value drops under the sticky masthead and the top card would pin behind
       it; the first term keeps it clear. 100svh rather than 100vh, so mobile
       browser chrome is counted. */
    --stack-top: max(
      calc(var(--sb-masthead-h-condensed) + clamp(10px, 1.4vh, 18px)),
      calc((100svh - var(--card-h) - var(--stack-n) * var(--stack-step)) / 2)
    );
  }

  /* THE SECTION'S DOT FIELD IS THE CLIENT'S dot-bg.png, BLED IN FROM THE LEFT.
     It replaced a CSS radial-gradient pattern, and the artwork is the better of
     the two: real squares at an uneven density that thins as it falls, rather
     than a perfectly regular grid of circles.

     PAINTED THROUGH THE ARTWORK AS A MASK, not laid on as an image. The file is
     light-coloured on transparency — on this ivory ground it would be invisible.
     Masking the page's ink through it gives dots that darken the paper, which is
     what texture actually does.

     TWO MASK LAYERS COMPOSITED with `intersect`: the artwork supplies the dot
     shapes and a left-to-right gradient supplies the bleed. The field is at full
     strength against the left edge and gone by two thirds across, so it frames
     the cards rather than sitting behind them.

     IT NEEDS NO CLIP. `inset: 0` against the relatively-positioned section is
     already exactly the section's box — see the warning on .asst about what
     adding `overflow: hidden` here does to the stack. */
  .asst__dots {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: -1;
    inset: 0;
    background: var(--as-ink);
    opacity: 0.16;
    -webkit-mask-image:
      url('../../../assets/icons/dot-bg.png'),
      linear-gradient(to right, #000 0%, #000 10%, transparent 64%);
    mask-image:
      url('../../../assets/icons/dot-bg.png'),
      linear-gradient(to right, #000 0%, #000 10%, transparent 64%);
    /* ONE COPY, NOT TILED. The artwork thins from top to bottom, so repeating it
       bands at every seam. Sized to a fixed height and anchored top-left, it
       plays its own fade out down the section and simply stops — which is the
       effect it was drawn for. */
    -webkit-mask-size: auto 1200px, 100% 100%;
    mask-size: auto 1200px, 100% 100%;
    -webkit-mask-position: left top, left top;
    mask-position: left top, left top;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat, no-repeat;
    mask-repeat: no-repeat, no-repeat;
    -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;
    mask-composite: intersect;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .asst__head { text-align: center; }

  .asst__eyebrow {
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--as-teal);
  }

  h2.asst__h {
    max-width: 22ch;
    margin: var(--sb-5) auto 0;
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(27px, 3.6vw, 48px);
    line-height: 1.06;
    letter-spacing: -0.036em;
    color: var(--as-ink);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .asst__list {
    max-width: 1080px;
    margin: clamp(38px, 5.4vh, 64px) auto 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* --- The row ------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /* ══ THE ROWS STACK, AND IT IS `position: sticky` DOING IT ═══════════════
     Each row pins a little lower than the one before, so scrolling collects the
     five into a deck rather than carrying them off the top of the screen.

     NO JAVASCRIPT, DELIBERATELY. The obvious build for this is a GSAP
     ScrollTrigger per card, and it buys nothing here: sticky is the browser's
     own implementation of exactly this, it runs on the compositor, it cannot
     desynchronise from the scroll position, and with a blocked script the
     section is still a perfectly good list. The one thing GSAP would add — the
     buried cards shrinking as they go under — is not worth a scroll handler and
     five triggers.

     THE OFFSET IS COMPUTED FROM --i, so the pile keeps its order whatever the
     step resolves to, and the stack is one declaration rather than five.

     ⚠ STICKY DIES INSIDE ANY ANCESTOR WITH `overflow` SET — hidden, auto or
     scroll. Nothing between here and the document currently sets it (checked:
     .asst, .u-container, .u-page-panel). If this section ever stops sticking,
     that is the first thing to look for; it fails silently and completely. */
  .astg {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--stack-top) + var(--i) * var(--stack-step));
    /* The scroll distance between one card arriving and the next. Bigger than
       the old gap because each card now needs a moment on its own before it is
       covered. */
    padding-bottom: clamp(30px, 6vh, 84px);
  }

  .astg:last-child { padding-bottom: 0; }

  /* ⚠ THE NODE MOVED INSIDE THE CARD, AND THE SIDE RAIL AND SPINE WENT WITH IT.
     A rail and a stack cannot both work: sticky pins the whole row, so five
     60px nodes piled at a 23px step showed 23px slivers of each other and read
     as a rendering fault rather than as a deck. The stack is the sequence device
     now — it is a better one, and the numeral below says where in the five you
     are, which a reader can no longer count once the cards overlap.

     SATURATED, not tinted. This is the one mark on the card big enough to carry
     the stage's colour — see the palette note on the page, where the washes were
     measured at 5 RGB units apart and abandoned as a differentiator. */
  .astg__meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(12px, 1.4vw, 18px);
  }

  .astg__node {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: var(--node);
    height: var(--node);
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-radius: var(--sb-r-full);
    background: var(--acc);
    color: #fff;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 5px color-mix(in srgb, var(--acc) 12%, transparent);
  }

  .astg__n {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--acc);
  }

  .astg__n i { font-style: normal; opacity: 0.45; }

  /* --- The panel ----------------------------------------------------------
     copy · side. The second column is what stops a full-width row from setting
     one very long line — the copy keeps a measure it can be read at, and the
     width left over carries the tags and the milestone. */
  /* ⚠ THE TORN BOTTOM EDGE IS GONE, at the client's request, and the card is
     back on the rounded border it had before it. Three things went with it,
     because all three existed only to serve the tear:
       · the `mask` that cut the zigzag;
       · `--tear`, and the bottom-padding allowance every cell paid it;
       · the `drop-shadow` filter on the row. A mask clips everything an element
         paints — a box-shadow included — so the shadow had to be a filter to
         follow the notches. With no mask, the shadow goes back on the panel
         where it belongs and costs nothing to composite.
     `overflow: hidden` replaces the mask's other job: clipping the stub's tint
     to the card's rounded corners. It is safe here — the sticky element is the
     ROW above this, and overflow on a descendant does not affect it. */
  .astg__panel {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: clamp(122px, 13vw, 176px) minmax(0, 1.16fr) minmax(0, 0.84fr);
    --pad-y: clamp(22px, 2.3vw, 32px);
    --pad-x: clamp(20px, 2.1vw, 30px);
    /* OPAQUE, not the glass the other panels on this page use. A translucent
       card in a stack shows the card beneath it through its own body copy. */
    background: #fff;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--acc) 16%, transparent);
    border-radius: clamp(18px, 1.7vw, 26px);
    /* THE INSET RULE IS WHY THE PILE IS LEGIBLE. Once cards stack, all a reader
       sees of the ones underneath is a 27px strip — and a strip of white card on
       an ivory page is a hairline, not a card. Painting the top edge in the
       stage's own accent turns the pile into a visible run of green → teal →
       blue → indigo → violet, so the deck shows the progression rather than
       hiding it. Inset shadow rather than border-top: a border would change the
       box's height and shift every card in the stack. */
    box-shadow:
      inset 0 3px 0 0 var(--acc),
      0 18px 44px -24px rgba(22, 24, 42, 0.34);
    transition:
      transform var(--sb-dur-image) var(--sb-ease),
      box-shadow var(--sb-dur-image) var(--sb-ease),
      border-color var(--sb-dur-image) var(--sb-ease);
  }

  .astg__panel:hover {
    transform: translateY(-4px);
    box-shadow:
      inset 0 3px 0 0 var(--acc),
      0 30px 66px -26px rgba(22, 24, 42, 0.42);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--acc) 34%, transparent);
  }

  /* ══ THE STUB ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Tinted in the stage's own accent and torn off down a perforation. The
     dashed border IS the perforation — a real one, not an image, so it lands on
     the pixel grid at every width and never blurs.

     ⚠ NO DOT ARTWORK IN HERE, AND THAT IS THE FIX FOR A REAL BUG. dot-bg.png is
     880×416 with its dots dense at the top and thinning downward. `cover` into
     a 176×305 stub scales it to 645×305, so the stub showed 27% of its width —
     and because the visible slice was the artwork's own top-left, it rendered as
     a block of squares in the corner with emptiness under it. A fragment, not a
     texture, and it read exactly like a broken background.

     No scale rescues it: fit the height and you still see a quarter of the
     width; tile it and the top-dense / bottom-sparse gradient bands at every
     seam, because the artwork is not seamlessly tileable. A 176px box is simply
     too small for this piece of art. It stays on the SECTION, which has the room
     to show it whole, and the stub keeps its accent wash — which was always the
     thing carrying the stage's colour anyway.

     ⚠ THE TINT IS THE WASH, NOT THE ACCENT. A saturated stub would need white
     type on five different hues and two of them are only 5.9:1 with it; the
     wash keeps the accent's own colour legible on top at 6.4:1 and up. */
  .astg__stub {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: clamp(12px, 1.4vw, 18px);
    padding: var(--pad-y) var(--pad-x) var(--pad-y);
    background: var(--acc-wash);
    border-right: 2px dashed color-mix(in srgb, var(--acc) 34%, transparent);
  }

  .astg__classes {
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.45;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--acc);
  }

  /* Pushed to the stub's foot, where a ticket prints its serial. */
  .astg__n { margin-top: auto; }

  /* --- The two paper halves ------------------------------------------------ */

  .astg__main { padding: var(--pad-y) clamp(24px, 2.6vw, 40px) var(--pad-y) clamp(24px, 2.6vw, 38px); }

  h3.astg__h {
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(21px, 2vw, 29px);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--as-ink);
  }

  /* The whole point of the rebuild. 58ch lands the body at 45–60 characters a
     line at every width this layout runs at — the old grid managed 17. */
  .astg__b {
    max-width: 58ch;
    margin-top: var(--sb-4);
    font-size: var(--sb-body-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--as-text);
  }

  /* --- Side column --------------------------------------------------------- */

  .astg__side {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: clamp(14px, 1.6vw, 20px);
    padding: var(--pad-y) clamp(24px, 2.6vw, 34px) var(--pad-y) 0;
  }

  /* ⚠ THE NUMERAL IS NOT IN THIS ROW ANY MORE — it moved to the stub. It had
     been absolutely positioned at the panel's top-right, and although the two
     never visually collided, its box sat INSIDE this label's box: a latent
     collision waiting for a longer label, and the reason the contrast checker
     read the numeral's indigo as this label's ground and reported 1.04:1 on
     type that is really 8:1. */
  .astg__l {
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--as-text);
  }

  .astg__items {
    margin: clamp(10px, 1.2vw, 14px) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .astg__item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 6px;
    padding-block: 4px;
    font-size: var(--sb-body-sm);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--as-ink);
  }

  .astg__nm { flex-shrink: 0; }

  /* THE LEADER. A flexible dotted rule between the item and its mark — it is
     what makes three entries of different lengths read as one ruled column
     rather than as three loose labels. */
  .astg__lead {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--sb-border-strong);
    translate: 0 -3px;
  }

  .astg__mk {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    background: var(--acc);
    border-radius: var(--sb-r-full);
  }

  /* The total row. margin-top: auto pins it to the panel's foot, so five cards
     of unequal copy still land their milestones on the same line — and the
     doubled dashed rule is the one a receipt puts above its total. */
  /* The gap above the label is 18-24px rather than 12-16, and the extra is not
     taste. At the tighter value the label's line box grazed the dashed rule
     above it, which put --sb-border-strong pixels inside the type's own box:
     harmless to read, but it made every contrast probe that landed there report
     3.7:1 on type whose real ground is white at 6.4:1. Separating them removes
     the graze rather than arguing with the checker about it. */
  .astg__ms {
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: clamp(18px, 2vw, 24px);
    border-top: 2px dashed var(--sb-border-strong);
  }

  .astg__ms-l {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--acc);
  }

  .astg__ms-v {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 4px;
    font-size: var(--sb-body-sm);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--as-ink);
  }

  /* The spine still ARRIVES under reduced motion — it just stops travelling.
     Removing it would take away the information that these five are one
     sequence; only the draw is motion. */
  /* The spine still ARRIVES under reduced motion — it just stops travelling.
     THE STACK GOES ENTIRELY. Cards sliding over one another as the page moves is
     precisely the kind of large-area motion that preference is asking to be
     spared, and unstacked they are a plain readable list with the spine running
     the whole way down — which is what the layout was before the stack and is
     complete on its own. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .astg {
      position: static;
      padding-bottom: clamp(22px, 3vh, 40px);
    }
    .astg__panel { transition: none; }
    .astg__panel:hover { transform: none; }
  }

  /* Below this the side column is under 220px and the tags start wrapping
     again — the exact fault the rebuild was for. The panel goes to one column
     and the side content sits under the copy. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    /* The panel goes single-column here and grows, so the pile it has to be
       centred against grows with it. */
    .asst { --card-h: 360px; }

    /* The stub becomes a strip across the top, and the perforation turns with
       it — a counterfoil torn off the short edge instead of the long one. */
    .astg__panel { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

    .astg__stub {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: center;
      gap: clamp(12px, 2.4vw, 20px);
      padding-bottom: var(--pad-y);
      border-right: 0;
      border-bottom: 2px dashed color-mix(in srgb, var(--acc) 34%, transparent);
    }

    .astg__n { margin-top: 0; margin-left: auto; }

    .astg__main { padding: var(--pad-y) var(--pad-x) 0; }
    .astg__side { padding: clamp(16px, 2vw, 22px) var(--pad-x) var(--pad-y); }
    .astg__tags { flex-direction: row; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .astg__ms { margin-top: 0; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .asst { padding-block: var(--sb-8) var(--sb-9); --node: 44px; }

    /* ⚠ NO STACK ON A PHONE. Measured at 390×844 the cards run to ~440px, so a
       pile of five needs more than half the screen before a single step is
